WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
A major company just dropped a free ai app that could change how millions chat every day. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 230. Still ahead are pictures you can tap because an ai built the buttons. Maps planning your day and quick bits. Alibaba, a big Chinese tech company, just launched a new ai model named Quen 3.5. Techju said, The company paired that model with a completely free app for regular people. An ai model is the program that learned from huge piles of tech

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.060
so it can guess what word should come next.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.960
Think of it like the world's smartest auto-complete.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.400
Quinn 3.5 is designed to compete directly with chat GPT,

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.840
the go-to chat bot for a lot of people.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.620
It offers similar chat, question answering,

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.840
and creative help, and the app does it without a subscription.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.320
When more companies fight for your attention,

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.300
the tools tend to get faster, smarter, and easier.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.260
A student stuck on homework or a teen hunting

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.200
for story ideas now has another place to turn

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.920
if one chatbot feels slow.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.860
Powerful AI is spreading beyond a single company, which can mean more choices and lower prices

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:07.260
later.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.800
The app is on phones today with no complicated setup.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.100
Search for Quinn in your usual app store, open it, and talk to it the way you would any

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:16.640
chatbot.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.520
Because it is free from the start, you can test more than one model without hitting a paywall.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:27.360
Give Quinn 3.5 the same prompt you already tried somewhere else, and notice which reply

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.200
actually helps your task. Trying that side-by-side is one of the fastest ways to see how different systems

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:40.049
think. Some AI systems can now place buttons and pop-up notes inside a drawing. The decoder reports

00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:45.929
instead of answering only with plain text. Picture a class poster with little paper flaps that hide

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:52.289
extra facts, where lifting a flap is what makes it fun. First, the model reads your request in ordinary

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:58.269
language. Next, it picks the pieces, like buttons or 3D effects, that would make the idea feel

00:01:58.269 --> 00:02:03.089
alive. After that, it writes instructions that tell those pieces what to do when you tap,

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:08.709
and it keeps the whole thing on one canvas. A canvas here is just a single shared space,

00:02:09.089 --> 00:02:14.709
like one sheet of poster board, so nothing spills into extra files. When the model plants a button

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:20.209
on your drawing, that tap is the screen version of lifting the flap. What you get feels like a tiny

00:02:20.209 --> 00:02:24.229
website or a mini-game you can play immediately, not a frozen picture.

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:29.609
The model learned those patterns from millions of existing web pages, so it can mix text,

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:33.469
graphics, and simple actions without you writing any instructions yourself.

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:38.329
You can ask for a choose your own adventure scene, watch choice buttons appear in the

00:02:38.329 --> 00:02:41.229
illustration, and tap them to see what happens next.

00:02:41.809 --> 00:02:45.809
The same steps work for a small dashboard that changes numbers when you press different

00:02:45.809 --> 00:02:46.409
options.

00:02:46.409 --> 00:02:52.449
Because everything stays on that one canvas, the result is easy to share or drop into something else.

00:02:52.929 --> 00:02:58.909
Interactive graphics like this once needed specialized tools, and now an everyday sentence can produce them.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:07.210
Inside Google Maps, the Verge noted, a chatbot called Ask Maps can build a full itinerary for one day or several days.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:11.230
An itinerary is just a ready-made schedule of where to go and when.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:17.190
Ask Maps is a chat window already in the app, powered by Gemini, so you type a request

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.690
and it answers without jumping to another program.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:24.310
It looks at places you might like and adds live details such as opening hours.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.250
Then it suggests the best order to visit everything.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.990
Short outings and longer multi-day trips are both fair game.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.230
Suggestions shift when real-time information changes.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:38.910
Weekend trips and a fun day out with friends are the kind of plans it is built to handle.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:44.130
Open the Google Maps app on your phone and tap the Ask Maps button powered by Gemini.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:48.930
Type this, plan a one-day trip to the city with coffee, a park, and lunch.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.530
You should get a ready-to-follow schedule you can tweak right away.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:58.110
Then ask a follow-up like, add a bookstore stop or make sure we finish before 6 p.m.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:00.330
and watch the plan update on the spot.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:05.250
Because the suggestions carry live details, you see which spots are open when you would

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:05.630
arrive.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:10.430
Directions, travel times, and saved places all stay in the same map screen.

00:04:11.365 --> 00:04:17.205
One tech thinker, in a post on X, suggests cars that steer themselves, with hotel room-style

00:04:17.205 --> 00:04:20.805
pods inside, for trips of 1,000 to 2,000 miles.

00:04:21.385 --> 00:04:26.085
Those pods could replace overnight flights, so you sleep in one city and wake up in another

00:04:26.085 --> 00:04:27.345
with no airport lines.

00:04:27.945 --> 00:04:32.965
The focus is comfort on the journey, turning travel time into rest time rather than transfers

00:04:32.965 --> 00:04:34.085
and security checks.

00:04:34.085 --> 00:04:35.525
That's it for today.

00:04:35.885 --> 00:04:39.385
Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:04:39.905 --> 00:04:43.065
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

00:04:43.405 --> 00:04:45.045
That's literally how learning works.

00:04:45.605 --> 00:04:48.645
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

